import{_ as t,o as i,c as s,a3 as o}from"./chunks/framework.BwzYn71E.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions (Output Priority)","description":"","frontmatter":{"translationSource":{"title":"阻尼理论与其修正和扩展（优先输出）","archivedAt":"2026-10-31","url":"https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=JN3ceSWPVQFlqvlfOsiR7k","scope":"page"},"translationLang":"en","head":[["link",{"rel":"canonical","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/damping-theory-revisions-and-extensions"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"en","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/damping-theory-revisions-and-extensions"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"zh-CN","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/zh/damping-theory-revisions-and-extensions"}],["link",{"rel":"alternate","hreflang":"x-default","href":"https://kokobird28144.github.io/endfield-skydoc/damping-theory-revisions-and-extensions"}]]},"headers":[],"relativePath":"damping-theory-revisions-and-extensions.md","filePath":"logistics/src/page-JN3ceSWPVQFlqvlfOsiR7k.md"}'),n={name:"damping-theory-revisions-and-extensions.md"};function a(r,e,p,c,d,l){return i(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<div class="logistics-content"><h1 id="damping-theory-revisions-and-extensions-output-priority" tabindex="-1">Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions (Output Priority) <a class="header-anchor" href="#damping-theory-revisions-and-extensions-output-priority" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions (Output Priority)&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Source page ID: <code>JN3ceSWPVQFlqvlfOsiR7k</code> ｜ <a href="https://docs.qq.com/aio/DTmluZ1diWlpQZldw?p=JN3ceSWPVQFlqvlfOsiR7k" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open in Tencent Docs</a></p></blockquote><p>Damping theory was created, organized, and maintained by @jnk, with extensive verification by @c, @苍孤狼, @苍蓝泥沼, and others.</p><p><span class="mark-red">Damping theory is outdated. For the latest theory, see </span><a href="/endfield-skydoc/introduction-to-analyzing-endfield-logistics-bugs">Introduction to Analyzing Endfield Logistics Bugs</a>. This document is retained as an archive of how the research developed.</p><p>Here we use the term <em>logistics path</em>:</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-8fcd80a97a7d923a.q90-3ab578c216c3cc2c.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p>Simply, these are all possible item routes from a factory output to the end of a logistics structure, shown in red. Paths not ending at factory inputs are open-ended logistics paths.</p><p><em>A logistics path&#39;s damping</em>:</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-db2412e650aacf6c.q90-900d91ad5f92aae5.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p>Damping is the number of logistics components along the path, including the factory input. An entire belt counts as one component; different Belt Bridge directions count as different components.</p><p>For compatibility with past calculations, we generally count downstream to upstream; counting the other way gives the same result.</p><p>Also for historical compatibility, everyday discussion generally subtracts one. This article does not.</p><p><em>Connection order</em>:</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-715107987777682b.q90-c03c4b9f3365c545.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p>An ordered data structure storing some placement-order information about downstream factories.</p><p>Specifically, when a logistics component connects to a factory input, append that input if absent.</p><p>When a component connected to a factory input is stored away, remove the input if it has no connections left.</p><p>This and subsequent images use black for connection order and red for damping/paths.</p><p>“In connection order” means traversing these factories from front to back (toward the end).</p><p><em>Output damping</em></p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-be4fb24402851a12.q90-c5f37be6c65c4982.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p>An output may have several paths, but selects only one path&#39;s damping as its own. Each output has one damping value; see damping theory for calculation and use.</p><p><em>Damping theory</em></p><p>A theory explaining occasional priority at factory outputs. Discussions of damping theory usually explain output priority.</p><p>First traverse upstream to downstream, breaking component-connection cycles: if an already-counted component appears, break the intervening connection. Then calculate all remaining paths.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-9e187546d03bd1d3.q90-24bda214b4c1536b.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p>Orange crosses mark connections broken to remove cycles.</p><p>Take factories in connection order and calculate damping for all paths connected to each factory. Each source factory output receives the minimum damping among its corresponding paths; subsequent calculation cannot overwrite it.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-24b791e74672f96d.q90-cc36e220e9f59478.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p>Connection order 1</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-ac408238404a797f.q90-b248825efe8a10c9.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p>Connection order 2</p><p>For outputs not yet calculated, use open-ended paths, again selecting the minimum without overwriting previous results.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-37f949889afbfbd5.q90-18cd325636e3f1af.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p>Among one factory&#39;s outputs, give all those beginning with non-Convergers the same maximum damping. This is called assimilation.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-a4944bcf0b8c8866.q90-0ce8c8e66b1f0e8e.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p>Assimilation effect</p><p>Finally, smaller damping gives higher priority level; earlier placement of the first component gives higher priority rank. Compare level first, then rank. If non-Converger-starting outputs take priority, rotate among them.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-1db17cff47b3f11b.q90-c2f4660c3abee22e.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p>Now admire the result.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-c90fe719ce5e3679.q90-6ad1c45baa6f0ddd.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p><em><strong>Points to note</strong></em></p><p>Priority does not block all non-priority routes. If priority routes cannot output everything, other routes carry the remainder.</p><p>Damping theory specifies factory-output priority, not logistics-path priority. Even high-damping paths may receive items from Splitters.</p><p>Not all outputs follow it, e.g. (Expanded) Reactor Crucibles.</p><p>Repeated tests show water Conduit Outlets follow it, while crucibles do not and show no output priority.</p><p>Damping theory is empirical, not an underlying principle.</p><p>Specifically, Reactor Crucible and Expanded Crucible solid output does not follow it. No liquid output except Conduit Outlet Manifolds follows it; those outputs create no priority.</p><p>With cycles, the theory is not always correct. Describe contradictory observations below.</p><p><strong>Bugs:</strong></p><p><s>When Convergers at some factory outputs belong to a loop satisfying special conditions, outputs rotate rather than prioritize; exact conditions need testing.</s> Verified: a co-rotating loop causes the 4*1, 1s-stall phenomenon, not a priority issue.</p><p>When a stash-output Converger belongs to a loop, priority may be abnormal and switchable. Research by @恒星泰斗:</p><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/attachments/关于顺旋的研究.docx-eb529678ae47fb09.docx">Attachment: Research into Clockwise Rotation.docx</a></p><blockquote><p>Original: 关于顺旋的研究.docx; size: 634595; objKey: <code>098c4738561242e8a3a31150bfbd5299</code>; local status: downloaded.</p></blockquote><p>@恒星泰斗 found the following; see the chat transcript.</p><details><summary>Unexpected open-ended logistics-path cases</summary><p>恒星泰斗 1:11:21</p><p>I tested one before: stash a has three routes to stash b, two using Splitters to connect to c and d. Damping is calculated in stash order; earlier stashes take priority.</p><p>Replacing cd with Belt Bridges sometimes gives similar behavior.</p><p>But belts, or Splitters separating multiple routes whose ends are all not stashes, do not work.</p><p>Also, an open-ended route directly connected to a stash without any preceding component behaves opposite to damping theory: later placement gives higher priority.</p><p>Adding any logistics component to that route restores damping-theory behavior.</p></details><p><span class="mark-red">Verified true. The open-ended belt description is revised in Revised Damping Theory at the end of this section.</span></p><p>@c found abnormal damping on open-ended paths: the middle route has highest priority below.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-ef37b02b4136e5bb.q90-8dbd470a87102115.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p>Verified true; the open-ended belt description is revised in Revised Damping Theory at the end.</p><p>@jnk noticed abnormalities involving open-ended paths in damping calculations for the 4n, 1s stall, below.</p><p><img src="/endfield-skydoc/logistics/src/assets/images/img-be837f85aea7a1a3.q90-5fdc998e1d7d6a14.webp" alt="Damping Theory, Revisions, and Extensions"></p><p>The component at an open-ended path&#39;s end can sometimes be regarded as stash-like.</p><p>Verified; the description is revised in Revised Damping Theory below.</p><p><a href="/endfield-skydoc/damping-theory-revised">Revised Damping Theory</a></p><p><strong>Extensions of Damping Theory</strong></p><p>After cycles are broken, damping usually matches layered BFS theory perfectly: movement calculation uses BFS from downstream, with input BFS visits within each layer following placement order.</p><p>Awaiting further extensions...</p></div>',1)])])}const h=t(n,[["render",a]]);export{g as __pageData,h as default};
